    "content": "> [!important] The Core Discipline: Deliberate Practice vs. Mere Production\n> Most creative work is unreflective production: you open a tool, start creating, solve problems as they arise, and finish when time runs out or it looks acceptable. \n> \n> **Deliberate practice is structurally different:**\n> 1. It isolates an explicit micro-skill (e.g., seeing major value masses in 5 seconds; writing a claim without adjectives).\n> 2. It sets an explicit criterion for success *before* starting.\n> 3. It operates in the **70–80% success zone** — if you succeed 10 out of 10 times, the drill is too easy; if you fail more than 3 times out of 10, the difficulty is overwhelming your feedback channel.\n> 4. It provides immediate, verifiable diagnostic feedback.\n>\n> If you only make full projects, you never isolate your bottlenecks. You merely practice your existing habits.\n\n\n---\n\n# Module 0 — How to Practice This Course\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n> [!important] The Missing Layer\n> This course trains judgment and authorship. But judgment does not improve from project work alone. It improves through **deliberate practice** — isolated, repeated, feedback-rich training of specific sub-skills.\n\n## Goal\n\nLearn to design practice rather than only complete assignments.\n\n## Core Distinction\n\n> [!warning] Practice is not production\n> **Production** aims at a deliverable. **Practice** aims at changing your reliable capacity. A practice session may produce nothing worth keeping. That is not failure. That is the point.\n\n## The Deliberate Practice Cycle\n\nEvery practice session follows this loop:\n\n1. **Name one micro-skill.** Not \"get better at composition\" — but \"place the focal point so the eye enters at the intended location.\"\n2. **Define an observable success criterion.** Something you can check without ambiguity.\n3. **Set difficulty just beyond current reliability.** You should fail roughly 2–4 reps out of 10.\n4. **Do focused reps.** Short, repeated, isolated from the full project.\n5. **Get immediate feedback.** Self-diagnosis against the criterion, or external eyes.\n6. **Correct one variable.**\n7. **Re-test.**\n8. **Space and interleave** with other skills.\n9. **Log what changed.**\n10. **Transfer to a new context.**\n\n## Practice Session Template\n\n| Phase | Time | Action | Output |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Intention | 5 min | Name micro-skill + criterion | Practice card |\n| Model | 10 min | Study anchor / demo | Mechanism notes |\n| Drill | 20–40 min | Focused reps | Practice artifacts |\n| Feedback | 10 min | Diagnose against criterion | Error list |\n| Correction | 10 min | Redo one rep | Improved rep |\n| Log | 5 min | Record | Practice log entry |\n\n## Difficulty Regulation\n\n> [!tip] The 70–80% Rule\n> If you succeed 9/10 reps, increase difficulty.\n> If you fail 6/10 reps, reduce difficulty or isolate a smaller skill.\n> The productive zone is roughly 70–80% success — enough failure to learn, enough success to reinforce.\n\n## Practice Log Fields\n\nEvery practice session records:\n\n- Date\n- Micro-skill\n- Success criterion\n- Reps completed\n- Difficulty level (1–5)\n- Errors observed\n- Correction applied\n- Result after correction\n- Next session focus\n\n> [!note] Logging Should Serve Practice, Not Replace It\n> If you spend more time logging than practising, you have the ratio wrong. One line per field is enough. The log is a thinking tool, not a compliance document.\n\n## Weekly Minimum (Core)\n\n| Activity | Frequency | Tier |\n|---|---|---|\n| Deliberate practice blocks (60–90 min) | 3× | Core |\n| Interleaving session (mix visual, writing, pipeline) | 1× | Core |\n| Transfer rep (remove one scaffold) | 1× | Core |\n| Non-instrumental attention session | 1× | Core |\n| Play session (unstructured making) | 1× | Core |\n| Rest / consolidation (no generation) | 1× | Core |\n\n> [!note] Why Interleaving Matters\n> Blocked practice feels more productive. Interleaved practice produces better transfer. Mixing visual, writing, and pipeline skills in one session forces your brain to select the right approach rather than simply repeat a motion.\n\n## Play Practice\n\n> [!important] Why Play Is Core\n> A course this structured can accidentally train rigidity. Play is the counter-practice. It is not optional. It is the space where you encounter the unexpected, the silly, the joyful, and the genuinely new.\n\n### What Counts as Play\n\nPlay is **unstructured making for its own sake**. It has:\n\n- no brief\n- no deliverable\n- no diagnosis\n- no criteria\n- no audience\n\nIt may produce something. It may produce nothing. Both are fine.\n\n### Examples\n\n- Drawing badly on purpose\n- Writing nonsense\n- Combining media that don't belong together\n- Making something with no intention of showing it\n- Following a stupid idea to see where it goes\n- Breaking a tool to see what happens\n- Making something that would embarrass you if anyone saw it\n\n### Rules\n\n- No evaluation\n- No logging (except perhaps one line: \"What did I enjoy?\")\n- No sharing unless you want to\n- No converting it into a project\n\n### Why This Matters\n\n> [!important] Play is not a reward for finishing work. It is an input to the work.\n> The material that surfaces from play often becomes the material that makes the work distinctive. Without play, the practice narrows to what you already know how to do.\n\n### Log\n\nAfter play, write one line:\n\n> What did I enjoy?\n\nDo not analyse it. Do not integrate it. Just record it.\n\n---"
