    "content": "> [!important] The Core Directive: What Is a Rep?\n> When the curriculum tells you to \"practise a micro-drill,\" it does not mean open Midjourney or Claude and generate cool things.\n> A **rep** is a single, timed, isolated execution of a micro-skill against an unambiguous pass/fail criterion.\n>\n> If you cannot state whether your last attempt passed or failed in 3 seconds, you did not do a rep — you were merely tinkering.\n\n---\n\n# Module 0B — How to Execute a Drill\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n> [!important] The Anatomy of a Drill\n> A drill is not a project milestone, an open brainstorm, or aesthetic exploration.\n> A drill is a cognitive treadmill designed to rewire your reflexive defaults.\n\n## 1. What a \"Rep\" Physically Means\n\nEvery rep is a discrete, 5-step loop lasting between 30 seconds and 10 minutes:\n\n1. **Choose Subject (15–30 sec):** Pick an unfamiliar image, sentence, or context. Unfamiliarity prevents memory shortcuts.\n2. **Execute Under Constraint (2–5 min):** Run the specific micro-protocol with timer running. Strict negative constraint: e.g. zero adjectives, 3 shapes only, 8 words max, 5 minutes hard cap.\n3. **Binary Self-Check (30–60 sec):** Apply the success criterion: PASS or FAIL. No \"maybe\" or \"pretty good.\" Did you violate the negative constraint or miss the condition?\n4. **Diagnose the Breakdown (30 sec):** If FAIL: isolate the exact mechanism that broke. Do not redo the entire rep — fix only the failed sentence, shape, or tension statement.\n5. **Log & Calibrate Difficulty (15 sec):** Mark ✓ or ✗. Check your running session rate. If 10/10 passing → tighten constraint immediately. If >4/10 failing → step down to warmup constraint.\n\n## 2. Where Do You Practice? (The 3 Surfaces)\n\nYou do not practice in a vacuum. Every drill runs on one of three concrete surfaces:\n\n- **Surface A: In-App Drill Runner:** Inside the Modality Studios in this app (e.g., M2 Studio → Drill Runner tab for Zero-Adjective or Massing; M1 Studio → Drill Runner tab for 5-in-30). Integrated rep counters, real-time adjective linter, zone feedback, and direct persistence.\n- **Surface B: Physical Sketchbook (Analogue):** An unruled paper notebook and a black marker or pen. Eliminates digital distractions and undo-temptation. Used for Visual Massing Sprints, 30-minute observational walks, and blind deconstructions.\n- **Surface C: Minimal Text Scratchpad:** Any plain text editor or the in-app studio textareas. Used for Sentence Surgery, Text Compression, and Diagnostic Ladder statements.\n\n## 3. The 70–80% Difficulty Rule: How to Calibrate\n\nThe single most common beginner mistake is either making the drill so easy that no learning occurs, or so hard that frustration replaces feedback.\n\n- **90–100% Pass (Too Easy):** You are confirming what you already know. Immediately apply the **Harder** or **Hardest** constraint: cut the time limit by 50%, pick a radically unfamiliar domain, or add a negative constraint.\n- **70–80% Pass (In the Learning Zone):** **Maintain this difficulty.** The 2–3 failures are where your brain is receiving error signals that drive neuroplastic adaptation.\n- **<50% Pass (Too Hard):** The constraint is overwhelming your perceptual channel. Drop immediately to the **Warmup** level: choose a simpler subject, give yourself 60 extra seconds, or isolate only one sub-rule.\n\n## 4. The 60–90 Minute Practice Block Architecture\n\nWhen your daily schedule says \"Practise: 60–90m\", here is how to partition your time:\n\n1. **0:00–0:05 (5 min) — Setup & Calibration:** Open the assigned drill in the Drill Runner or sketchbook. Read target skill and success criterion. Prepare 5–10 reference subjects.\n2. **0:05–0:60 (50–55 min) — Continuous Rep Sprint:** Execute 5 to 10 reps back-to-back. Keep individual rep durations strict. Grade each rep PASS or FAIL immediately upon completion. Adjust constraint upward or downward based on the 70–80% rule.\n3. **0:60–0:75 (15 min) — Error Audit & Mechanism Discovery:** Review your failed reps. Ask: \"What was the recurring failure mode?\" Write 1–2 sentences in the session discovery note.\n4. **0:75–0:80 (5 min) — Save & Complete:** Click \"Save & Complete Session\" in the Drill Runner. Mark the Daily Practise step as completed.\n\n## 5. Worked Examples: Three Modalities\n\n### Example A: Visual Massing Sprint (M2)\n- **Subject:** Photograph of a crowded train station.\n- **Constraint:** Flash for 5 seconds, sketch from memory in 90 seconds. 3–5 solid shapes only. No lines.\n- **Passing Rep:** A sketch containing 4 dark/light geometric silhouettes showing the roof diagonal, the dark floor crowd block, the central bright skylight, and the train mass. Hierarchy is immediately legible at arm's length.\n- **Failing Rep:** A sketch showing the clock face hands, window mullions, and individual people's coats. (Detail was drawn instead of mass structure).\n\n### Example B: Zero-Adjective Observation (M2)\n- **Subject:** AI-generated render of an abandoned greenhouse.\n- **Constraint:** 4 sentences. Zero evaluative adjectives. Structural properties only.\n- **Passing Rep:** \"A rusted iron barrel-vault grid spans twelve meters across the frame. Shattered rectangular glass panels leave open polygonal gaps through which vertical vines enter. The ground plane is covered in thirty centimeters of decayed matter. Light enters at a sixty-degree diagonal from the upper-right quadrant.\"\n- **Failing Rep:** \"A gorgeously moody, melancholic greenhouse stands abandoned. The stunning green vines create an eerie, poetic atmosphere with dramatic lighting.\" (Contains 6 evaluative shortcuts; rejected by linter).\n\n### Example C: Sentence Surgery (M3)\n- **Subject:** \"The rapid proliferation of generative artificial intelligence creates significant challenges for traditional intellectual property frameworks.\"\n- **Constraint:** Produce 5 distinct structural rewrites: (1) Compressed (<8 words), (2) Fragmented (3 short), (3) Question, (4) Inverted, (5) Subordinated.\n- **Passing Rep:** Produces 5 distinct versions with identifiable reader stance shifts.\n- **Failing Rep:** Produces 5 versions that merely swap synonyms without changing syntax or reader tension.\n\n## 6. What Goes Into Your Practice Log After\n\nYour practice log should take **less than 2 minutes** to fill out:\n\n- **Drill ID & Date:** Zero-Adjective Observation — Day 3\n- **Reps Done:** 8 reps (6 passed, 2 failed = 75% — In Zone)\n- **Key Breakdown:** Failed reps 3 and 7 because I used \"dramatic\" and \"delicate\" to describe shadow edges.\n- **Correction Applied:** Substituted \"hard-edged contrast boundary\" and \"low-value gradient\".\n- **Difficulty Adjustment:** Maintain standard difficulty tomorrow.\n\n---"
