    "content": "> [!important] Honesty Over Inflation\n> Theoretical familiarity is not operational competence. Scoring a 3 or 4 because you *understand the concept intellectually* blinds you to where your reflexes fail under friction.\n> A low score (1 or 2) is not a failure; it is your highest-leverage growth target for Part I.\n\n\n---\n\n# Week 0 — Baseline & Practice Contract\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n> [!important] Do this before Week 1\n> Without a baseline, you cannot measure progress. Without a practice contract, you cannot hold yourself accountable. Without an access audit and income plan, you cannot plan realistically.\n\n## 1. Baseline Diagnostic\n\nComplete one short exercise in each competency. Score yourself honestly using the rubric scale in [[#Appendix C — Rubrics|Appendix C]].\n\n| Competency | Exercise | Score (1–4) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Problem-finding | Identify 3 problems worth pursuing from 30 min of observation | | |\n| Positionality | State whether you have standing to pursue one of them | | |\n| Observation | Describe an image / passage without evaluative adjectives | | |\n| Premise | Generate 3 premises from one observation | | |\n| Specification | Write a 1-page brief for a self-chosen premise | | |\n| Diagnosis | Diagnose the highest-level problem in an unfamiliar work | | |\n| Curation | Rank 5 works and explain criteria | | |\n| Systems | Document a workflow you already use | | |\n| Prediction | Predict what would break if one variable changed, with confidence | | |\n| Direction | Make and defend one consequential decision under constraint | | |\n| Authorship | State what you repeatedly pursue and refuse | | |\n| Transfer | Apply one skill to an unfamiliar medium | | |\n\n> [!warning] Do Not Skip This\n> The baseline is not a test. It is a map. Without it you will not know which phase to weight more heavily, which skills to practice more, or whether you have actually changed.\n\n> [!example] What a Baseline Entry Looks Like\n> **Competency:** Premise\n> **Exercise:** Generate 3 premises from one observation\n> **Score:** 2 (Emerging)\n> **Notes:** I generated three topics, not premises. \"Loneliness in cities\" is a subject, not a proposition with tension. I need to practice moving from observation → question → tension → premise.\n\n## 2. Tool and Access Audit\n\n| Category | What I have | Cost | Limitations | Fallback |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Visual generation | | | | |\n| Writing generation | | | | |\n| Drawing / painting tools | | | | |\n| Node-based / pipeline tools | | | | |\n| Reference management | | | | |\n| Version control | | | | |\n| Research tools | | | | |\n| Audio / time-based tools | | | | |\n| Spatial / installation tools | | | | |\n| Hardware | | | | |\n| Budget per month | | | | |\n| Time per week | | | | |\n\n## 3. Income and Sustainability Plan\n\n> [!important] The Intensive Is Unpaid Study\n> The Core intensive is 18–25 hrs/week. Deepening extends that to 35. Neither is compatible with full-time employment without adjustment. You must decide, before Week 1, how this period is funded.\n\n| Field | My answer |\n|---|---|\n| Funding source for the 12 weeks | |\n| Reduction in paid work | |\n| Savings or support | |\n| Monthly burn rate | |\n| Runway (weeks) | |\n| Plan if funding runs out early | |\n| Minimum income needed to continue | |\n| Part-time option (see [[#Appendix O — Working Across Intensity Levels\\|Appendix O]]) | |\n\nIf full-time intensive is not possible, use [[#Appendix O — Working Across Intensity Levels|Appendix O]] to run the course at reduced intensity without breaking the scaffolding sequence.\n\n## 4. Ethics and Disclosure Agreement\n\nWrite and sign your own agreement:\n\n> I will not generate:\n> - [ ] likenesses of real people without consent\n> - [ ] content that impersonates a named creator\n> - [ ] material intended to deceive about its origin\n> - [ ] work that violates copyright or license\n> - [ ] content that targets or harms identifiable groups\n> - [ ] other: _______________\n>\n> I will determine disclosure from:\n> - [ ] the applicable law\n> - [ ] the contract or publication standard\n> - [ ] the materiality of AI's contribution\n> - [ ] the audience's reasonable expectations\n> - [ ] platform or institutional policy\n> - [ ] other: _______________\n\n## 5. Personal Practice Plan\n\n| Field                                              | My answer |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| Primary medium focus                               |           |\n| Secondary medium focus                             |           |\n| Tertiary mode (if any: sound, time-based, spatial) |           |\n| Weekly hours available                             |           |\n| Best time of day for practice                      |           |\n| Biggest weakness (from baseline)                   |           |\n| First micro-skill to train                         |           |\n| How I will get feedback                            |           |\n| Who is in my calibration network                   |           |\n| What I will do when I fail                         |           |\n| What I will do when I succeed                      |           |\n| What play will look like for me                    |           |\n\n## 6. Practice Log Setup\n\nCreate a single file or notebook titled `Practice Log — [Your Name]`. Use the template in [[#Appendix L — Operational Templates|Appendix L]].\n\n> [!tip] Keep It Simple\n> Do not build an elaborate system. One file, one table, one row per session. The practice is the point; the log is a shadow of it.\n\n## 7. Calibration Network Agreement\n\n> [!important] Not Just Critics\n> The people who help you calibrate are not merely critics. Some represent audience, some represent craft, some represent domain knowledge, some represent ordinary perception. Build the network deliberately.\n\n| Name | Role | What they calibrate | Availability | Preferred mode |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | Audience | | | |\n| | Craft expert | | | |\n| | Domain knowledge | | | |\n| | Ordinary perception | | | |\n| | Peer / co-creator | | | |\n\n> [!tip] If You Cannot Build a Network\n> Use online communities, post work publicly and ask specific questions, hire a coach for monthly sessions, or use structured self-critique with the protocol in [[#Appendix J — Feedback, Critique & Self-Calibration|Appendix J]].\n\n## 8. Collaboration Readiness\n\nIf your practice involves working with peers as co-creators rather than clients or critics, record:\n\n| Field                                | My answer |\n| ------------------------------------ | --------- |\n| Current or planned collaborators     |           |\n| How authorship will be negotiated    |           |\n| How decision rights will be split    |           |\n| How taste conflicts will be resolved |           |\n| How revenue and credit will be split |           |\n| What happens if the partnership ends |           |\n\nSee [[#Module 14 — Collaborative Direction|Module 14]] for the full protocol.\n\n## 9. Accessibility and Wellbeing Needs\n\n| Need          | Accommodation |\n| ------------- | ------------- |\n| Physical      |               |\n| Sensory       |               |\n| Cognitive     |               |\n| Mental health |               |\n| Financial     |               |\n| Time          |               |\n| Language      |               |\n| Other         |               |\n\n---"
