    "content": "> [!important] The Statistical Gravity Well of AI\n> AI models are probability engines trained to predict the median consensus. When you prompt with adjectives like \"cinematic\", \"dramatic\", or \"thoughtful\", you surrender directional control and summon the median statistical average of the training data.\n> \n> True creative direction does not ask the model to \"make it good\". It provides **geometric constraints, directional lighting angles, premise tensions, and negative refusals**.\n\n### The 6-Question Provenance Audit:\nWhenever you evaluate a machine-generated output:\n1. **What is it apparently trying to do?** (Identify intended function).\n2. **Which decisions are explicit?** (What did you deliberately specify?).\n3. **Which decisions appear to be defaults?** (What did the model fill in from training bias?).\n4. **Which decision most determines whether it succeeds?** (Locate the critical lever).\n5. **Who or what made that decision?** (Human vs Model vs Random Seed).\n6. **What would a human director need to decide next?** (Your actionable intervention).\n\n\n---\n\n# Module 1 — The Judgment Gap\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Goal\n\nUnderstand what changes when generation becomes cheap, and distinguish production capability from judgment capability.\n\n## Core Ideas\n\n- automation of production is not automation of purpose\n- generation is not authorship\n- novelty is not significance\n- fluency is not clarity\n- plausibility is not truth\n- beauty is not coherence\n- variation is not exploration unless the variation has a question behind it\n- **a question is not exploration until it changes what you do**\n- a generated choice still has a source, whether human, model-default, dataset convention, or system constraint\n\n> [!important] The Reciprocal Principle\n> \"Variation is not exploration unless the variation has a question behind it\" is necessary but not sufficient.\n>\n> The reciprocal is also true: **a question is not exploration until it changes what you do.**\n>\n> Without this, intellectualised problem-finding becomes another form of procrastination. A learner can generate fifty fascinating premises without ever committing to one.\n\n## Procedure — Finding the Human Decision\n\nTake a generated output.\n\nAsk:\n\n1. What is it apparently trying to do?\n2. Which decisions are explicit?\n3. Which decisions appear to be defaults?\n4. Which decision most determines whether it succeeds?\n5. Who or what made that decision?\n6. What would a human director need to decide next?\n\nRecord the result.\n\n> [!example] What This Looks Like in Practice\n>\n> **Output:** A generated image of a lone figure on a city street at night, neon reflections in puddles.\n>\n> **What it is apparently trying to do:** Evoke urban loneliness or noir atmosphere.\n>\n> **Explicit decisions:** The figure's position (center-left), the color palette (cyan/magenta), the reflective surface.\n>\n> **Apparent defaults:** The cinematic aspect ratio, the shallow depth of field, the \"lonely figure in neon city\" trope itself. These are model defaults, not chosen.\n>\n> **Most determining decision:** The choice to center the figure. This makes it a portrait of isolation rather than a portrait of the city.\n>\n> **Who made it:** The model made it by default. The human did not choose it.\n>\n> **Next human decision:** Does this image want to be about the figure or about the city? The current composition says figure, but the title and the puddles say city. The human needs to decide which one the work is actually about.\n\n## Constraint Drill\n\nDescribe an output without using evaluative adjectives such as good, bad, beautiful, ugly, impressive, weak, strong, polished, or boring.\n\nThen diagnose it.\n\nThis separates observation from judgment.\n\n## Transfer Test\n\nTake an unfamiliar output from a medium you do not normally work in.\n\nIdentify:\n\n- the likely intention\n- the highest-level unresolved problem\n- the next decision you would make\n\nNo ladder is displayed.\n\n---"
