    "content": "> [!important] The Neutral-Prose Subtraction Diff\n> If you strip out rhetorical flourish and adjectives from an essay and nothing of substance remains, the piece has no positionality. Voice is created by what you refuse to say and the rhythm of your claims.\n\n\n---\n\n# Module W1 — Writing Observation & Diagnosis\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild the reading ability required to judge generated prose.\n\n## Writing Diagnostic Ladder\n\n1. **Intent** — what is the piece trying to do?\n2. **Claim / premise** — what is it asserting, asking, or proposing?\n3. **Argument / structure** — how is the claim developed?\n4. **Voice / stance** — who is speaking and from what position?\n5. **Rhythm** — how does the prose move?\n6. **Sentence / paragraph logic** — do local units cohere?\n7. **Clarity** — can the reader follow the thought?\n8. **Mechanics** — are the basic conventions working?\n9. **Polish** — are surface choices supporting the whole?\n\n## Close-Reading Protocol\n\nFor one strong human-written passage:\n\n1. mark where the writer makes a turn\n2. mark what is omitted\n3. mark where the rhythm changes\n4. state what the reader is made to anticipate\n5. paraphrase the underlying claim\n6. rewrite the passage in neutral prose\n7. compare what disappeared\n\nThe purpose is to discover that voice is not decoration added after meaning.\n\n> [!example] What a Close Reading Looks Like\n>\n> **Passage:** The opening of Joan Didion's \"The White Album.\"\n>\n> **Turn:** The first turn is in the second sentence: \"I was supposed to be writing a piece for the *Saturday Evening Post*.\" The first sentence establishes a scene; the second reveals a task. The reader's expectation shifts from atmosphere to obligation.\n>\n> **Omission:** Didion never explains what the piece was supposed to be about. The absence is the point — the failure to write it is the subject.\n>\n> **Rhythm change:** The prose moves from long, observational sentences to short, declarative ones when the narrator's mental state deteriorates. The rhythm mirrors the content.\n>\n> **Anticipation:** The reader is made to anticipate a breakdown. The opening's calm is ominous because we know Didion's work is often about the failure of calm.\n>\n> **Underlying claim:** \"I was not able to make sense of what was happening, and the attempt to make sense of it is the story.\"\n>\n> **Neutral rewrite:** \"I was in Los Angeles in 1968. I was supposed to write an article but I couldn't. Many things happened. I couldn't understand them.\"\n>\n> **What disappeared:** The tension between the professional obligation and the personal incapacity. The sense that the writing itself is the attempt to hold it together. The voice — the specific way Didion makes you feel her intelligence as a kind of vulnerability.\n\n## Transfer Test\n\nTake a piece in an unfamiliar genre.\n\nIdentify the author's likely intention, the strongest structural decision, and one consequential omission without rewriting anything.\n\n---"
