    "content": "> [!important] Beyond Compliance\n> Provenance records don't just satisfy copyright; they document what decisions were authored by human judgment vs generated by algorithms.\n\n\n---\n\n# Module 7 — Provenance Begins\n\n[[#Table of Contents|↑ Back to TOC]]\n\n## Goal\n\nBegin recording the human contribution at the point where decisions are first made, not retroactively after the work is done.\n\n> [!important] Why Provenance Starts in Part II\n> Provenance is usually treated as a delivery concern. It belongs earlier. The first decisions about premise, reference, and constraint already determine what is authored and what is inherited from a tool. Recording them from the start makes the record accurate and the authorship legible.\n\n## What to Record\n\nFor every premise and brief produced in Part II, record:\n\n| Field | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Premise origin | Where did it come from? |\n| Human contribution | What did I decide, reject, transform? |\n| Tool contribution | What was generated, suggested, or scaffolded? |\n| Reference sources | What did I study or borrow principles from? |\n| Licence / consent | Any relevant restrictions on reference material |\n| Version of tool | Which model or tool, at which point in its evolution? |\n\n## Procedure\n\n1. Open the provenance record template from [[#Appendix L — Operational Templates|Appendix L]].\n2. Fill it in at the moment of premise selection.\n3. Update it when the brief is written.\n4. Revisit it before each diagnosis.\n\n## Why This Matters\n\n> [!important] Provenance Is Not Paperwork\n> It is a record of authorship. The question it answers is not only \"Where did the material come from?\" but **\"What did the human actually decide, transform, construct, reject, and contribute?\"**\n\n> [!example] What a Provenance Record Looks Like\n>\n> **Premise origin:** I noticed a pattern on my commute — people holding their phones at increasingly steep angles as if trying to hide them. I asked why. The premise came from that question.\n>\n> **Human contribution:** I chose the question. I rejected three other interpretations of the pattern. I chose the tension (visibility vs. concealment). I wrote the premise.\n>\n> **Tool contribution:** None at this stage. The premise was entirely human.\n>\n> **Reference sources:** None yet. References will be chosen when the brief is written.\n>\n> **Licence / consent:** N/A at this stage.\n>\n> **Version of tool:** N/A at this stage.\n\n## The Simplicity Principle\n\n> [!tip] Keep Provenance Light\n> The provenance record does not need to be a legal document. It needs to answer one question: what did I actually decide? If you can answer that in a few sentences, the record is complete. The full template in Appendix L is for professional contexts where the record may need to be produced later. For daily practice, a short form is enough.\n\n## Transfer Test\n\nGiven a completed brief from someone else, reconstruct what you can and cannot infer about the human contribution.\n\nState which parts are legible and which are missing.\n\n---"
