    "content": "> [!important] The Final Capstone\n> The ultimate test of your creative independence: 100% self-found problem, complete provenance, public release, and professional defense.\n\n\n---\n\n## Project 12 — Capstone (Month 9)\n\n**Type:** Hybrid\n**Duration:** 4 weeks\n**Estimated workload:** 30–35 hrs/week\n**Prerequisites:** All intensive phases, Projects 7–11\n\n### Goal\n\nProduce a full campaign or editorial package — visual and textual — that demonstrates the full curriculum as an integrated practice. The capstone is the portfolio's centrepiece and the evidence that the director can run a project end-to-end without supervision.\n\n### Brief\n\nYou are directing a complete creative package for a client, publication, or project of your choice. The package must include a visual component and a textual component, integrated under a single authorial position. The package must demonstrate the full pipeline: brief, specification, generation, diagnosis, curation, direction, refusal, and delivery.\n\n### Deliverables\n\n| Deliverable | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Working brief** | Intention, constraints, references, acceptance criteria, rejection conditions, risk assessment for both components |\n| **Visual pipeline** | ComfyUI node graph, fully documented |\n| **Writing pipeline** | Prompt chain, fully documented |\n| **First sets** | 10 visual outputs and 5 drafts, each diagnosed |\n| **Second sets** | 10 visual and 5 textual revised outputs, each diagnosed, with one accepted each and three rejected each, justified |\n| **Final package** | Visual component (key visual, campaign assets, or image set) and textual component (editorial, campaign copy, or script), integrated |\n| **Authorial position** | One page. Private and public. What is this work for? |\n| **Revenue model** | Licensing, service, or hybrid. Specific mechanism. Three client profiles. |\n| **Decision log** | Minimum 30 entries, each naming the decision, the level, the alternative, and the outcome |\n| **Written defense** | One page, naming the visual and writing levels, the decisions, and the alternatives |\n| **Refusal statement** | One page. The categories of refusal practised, with examples from the six months of work. |\n| **External critique record** | One structured critique session on the full package |\n| **Portfolio integration** | The capstone becomes the centrepiece of the portfolio from Phase VI |\n\n### Package Requirements\n\nThe package must demonstrate:\n\n- A single authorial position across visual and textual components.\n- The visual and textual components share an intention.\n- The components are integrated, not merely combined.\n- The package can be defended as a whole, not just as parts.\n- The decision log reconstructs every major decision across both components.\n- The refusal statement names the boundaries of the practice.\n\n### Pipeline Requirements\n\nBoth pipelines must be documented. The integration of the two pipelines must be explained in the decision log. The package must include at least one LoRA or IP-Adapter, at least one ControlNet conditioning layer, a voice anchor, a draft gate, and a fact gate.\n\n### External Critique Requirements\n\n- What is the package trying to do?\n- Is the authorial position clear?\n- Do the visual and textual components share an intention?\n- Does the integration work, or does one component dominate?\n- At what level does the package fail?\n- Does the package share the AI default signature in either medium?\n- Would you hire this director? Why or why not?\n\n### Standing Inquiry Integration\n\n- **Weekly:** One sentence in the decision log.\n- **Monthly:** One page answering \"What is this work for?\"\n- **Quarterly:** A written revision of the answer, read against previous quarterly answers.\n- **Annual:** A full synthesis, read against all previous entries.\n\n### Mastery Gate\n\nThe capstone is complete when:\n\n- The brief scores 3 or higher on the Intent Architecture rubric.\n- Both pipelines score 3 or higher on their respective rubrics.\n- The authorial position scores 3 or higher on the Authorship as Position rubric.\n- The package demonstrates a single authorial position across both components.\n- The decision log reconstructs every major decision.\n- The written defense names both levels, decisions, and alternatives.\n- The refusal statement names the categories and provides examples.\n- Every verifiable claim is verified.\n- The external critique is logged and diagnosed.\n- The capstone is integrated into the portfolio.\n\n### Common Failure Modes\n\n- **Two projects, not one.** The visual and textual components do not share an intention. Fix: state the intention in one sentence. If it takes two, it is two projects.\n- **One component dominates.** The visual or textual component is clearly stronger. Fix: the integration must be balanced. Test: which component carries the package? If one, the integration has failed.\n- **No authorial position.** The package demonstrates skill but not a position. Fix: the position must be stated and demonstrated. The portfolio is the evidence.\n- **No refusal statement.** The director has not articulated their boundaries. Fix: the refusal statement is not optional. It is the evidence of a practice.\n- **No external critique.** The director has not tested the package against outside eyes. Fix: the critique is required. Self-assessment alone has blind spots.\n\n---\n\n**End of Reading List and Supervised-Practice Project Briefs.**\n\nThe reading list provides the primary and supplementary sources cited throughout the curriculum, with specific extracts named for each. The six project briefs provide the full specifications for the supervised practice period, each with deliverables, requirements, decision log requirements, external critique requirements, standing inquiry integration, mastery gates, and common failure modes."
